rgins":0.0,"_gate_fund_growth":null,"_gp_fund_growth":null,"_score_fund_growth":0.0,"_gate_shrhldr_yield":0.009254498771311669,"_gp_shrhldr_yield":false,"_score_shrhldr_yield":21.4,"_gate_share_shrink":0.006700695928077538,"_gp_share_shrink":false,"_score_share_shrink":41.6,"_gate_sbc_dilution":0.011717495987158908,"_gp_sbc_dilution":true,"_score_sbc_dilution":80.5,"_gate_insider_own":0.11116,"_gp_insider_own":true,"_score_insider_own":74.1,"_gate_insider_buying":null,"_gp_insider_buying":null,"_score_insider_buying":null,"pf":[],"description_full":"Cinemark Holdings, Inc., together with its subsidiaries, engages in the theatrical exhibition business. It operates theatres in the United States and Latin America. Cinemark Holdings, Inc. was founded in 1984 and is headquartered in Plano, Texas.","ceo_bio":"Mr. Sean  Gamble | CEO, President & Director | Age 50 | Compensation: $4.3M (FY2025)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 8K people. Revenue per employee of $369K ranks in the bottom quartile among Communication Services peers. Each employee generates $8K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $4K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 12\u00d7 revenue per employee (reasonable relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (4/10).","financial_summary":["Cinemark Holdings Inc Cinemark  is a mid-cap communication services business with a $4.5B market cap on $3.1B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +8% over the past three years and +1% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 9% against a 9% cost of capital, which means the business is destroying value with every dollar it reinvests.","The business throws off $71M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 2 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.0\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and thin 2.1\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Shareholder yield is only 0.9%, a thin return of capital, and insiders hold 11% of the shares, so management thinks like owners because it is one \u2014 the strongest alignment there is, and profit capture is only 0.5\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Trading near 52-week highs \u2014 the market has already recognised the story","sev":"amber","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.11445279866332499},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Elevated short interest (11.1% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting the other way","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Communication Services ETF trading well below its long-term trend","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Above-average market volatility (VIX 26)","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Late-cycle dynamics increase the probability of a slowdown","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Advertising-cycle exposure and content/regulatory risk \u2014 a single platform policy shift or ad-spend contraction cascades through the P&L quickly.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"Cord-cutting acceleration and audience fragmentation \u2014 legacy distribution bundles unravel faster than subscription revenue can replace them.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"},{"text":"AI-generated content commoditisation \u2014 as creation costs collapse toward zero, content libraries lose pricing power and differentiation narrows.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Healthy payout ratio leaves room for both dividend growth and reinvestment"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"Cinemark Holdings (CNK) Record